            <ArticleTitle>Multiple spontaneously occurring coronary artery-left ventricular communications: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>208-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A search of the literature revealed that spontaneous coronary artery-left ventricular communications have only rarely been reported. These fistulae are frequently associated with angina pectoris which has been attributed to a ventricular steal phenomenon. The patient described herein presented with angina pectoris and was found to have multiple coronary arterioventricular communications without significant coronary atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
